{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.5,"content_digest":"0c3b613a49afa3a4f7d5b8074669300c0fc211e8caf08ab3607a061bcd9fcc5b","created_at":"2026-08-22T16:07:21+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"44385465868cc1637ec6ecaee1fb6ed4f2715517177adcc9673be06e9ea5f7cd","input_tokens":5588,"latency_ms":34099,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"7e370c8d07f17b1a21884c0001b2dc6529064e3323b6888a7898e9a96d3a293b","output_tokens":2645,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:712eb99f89586366347d1ca5","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:transportability","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0N3K0JHVCDSGTREXRGV5TGY","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle's claim that a stable file-level score is insufficient without declaring reader, task, and representation parallels the transportability requirement that generalization across environments requires explicit invariance assumptions rather than superficial label or score similarity. The alignment is conceptual and partial because the principle concerns measurement invariance rather than causal effect transport.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T16:07:21+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.65,"content_digest":"30a6e3ef503b9e3066cc71d7e7e0be2a831c98a900bc4b099b4986b219ace932","created_at":"2026-08-22T14:35:51+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"1fbe591198af42f04ecc4708a6b345fb925cddc902b7bb2a0771383e2ea32c75","input_tokens":6266,"latency_ms":40605,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"556517956d5013048a837743676c51b26129d9dcf7f156f2890a4356aa40bd75","output_tokens":2572,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:7bc1430fb4ceaf65a178029a","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:measurement-theory-ladenness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0MYB98R9KRZ2MXJEKVCCAGY","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle explicitly tests whether the observed correlation is an artifact of measurement method or parcellation choice, acknowledging that measured network properties depend on operational definitions and reconstruction choices. This is consistent with the measurement-theory-ladenness meta-principle, though the meta-principle does not by itself establish the empirical correlation.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T14:35:51+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.88,"content_digest":"e1e1eb146e683abe4b1649cf78216ce683fbc766788478769a95c757f7b76045","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:30:04+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:a9b0a4994e9b3505f24554ed","meta_principle_id":"meta:scientific-discovery:benchmark-saturation-leakage","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:general:01KZXKJ0R7EQMEKBVWSSTKXMDP","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle is a concrete case of the meta-principle: public benchmark contamination inflates LLM accuracy on standard multiple-choice questions, and the NOTO variation reduces reliance on memorized answers, thereby restoring some measurement validity. The meta-principle's conditions (contamination, saturation, repeated optimization) and variables (benchmark score vs intended capability) match the principle's public/private dataset contrast and accuracy comparison.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:30:04+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"015d777b3f1834c6f084f6272f8e1899385976bf11bba4b52c3c22a07c3e9dc5","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:38:39+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"b6dce5e7a69620bb237895730e44c05162765d1e0ec576d848902e69e0df6cab","input_tokens":3700,"latency_ms":29560,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"13e11a0174a78cbedde53b2b58f9cb2645621eef5645fdc2c8e2847b3c3db59d","output_tokens":2433,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:d5a25037a2db0f4fe4550681","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:inductive-bias","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NC86NSAEJ18EFFSX7BZ29V","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that generalization from finite data requires inductive bias and explicitly lists data augmentation as one such bias. The principle is a concrete instance: label-preserving transformations encode invariances that reduce overfitting when training data are limited. The relation is explanatory rather than evidential; the empirical claim stands on its own evidence.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:38:39+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
